It's Larry, Daryl, and Daryl.

247
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
I always get the little girl up when I get into town.

248
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
How will you find her?

249
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
I'd recognize her eyes.

250
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Any place.

251
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Oh, I refuse to watch this.

252
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
The place most of these kids end up is the pokey.

253
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Of course not.

254
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
They're good kids.

255
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
They end up at the terrace at the lake.

256
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Not that I go to places like that.

257
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Not that I would have a healthy relationship.

258
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:55,000
But I've heard of whatever the lieutenant says, sir.

259
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
I'm with him, sir.

260
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:10,000
The White Rock Cafe where Whitey comes to drink and dance.

261
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Wonderful, wonderful. Thank you, boys.

262
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
Rock and roll can lead to meeting other people with similar interests.

263
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Yeah.

264
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Brought to you by White Beer.

265
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
There's a trailer park of flavor in every bottle.

266
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
Ah, will there be anything else?

267
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
Right now I can't afford what I'm thinking.

268
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Oh, shut up. Not you now.

269
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
I got news for you. You never will.

270
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Say, there is one thing.

271
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
I was wondering if you could tell me how to...

272
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
What's this word in the state's directions? Emotes?

273
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
I have written on writing gear. Let's see.

274
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Could you tell me how to get to...

275
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
Polly Holiday's house?

276
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
First base with you tonight?

277
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:57,000
I'd like to apologize for the entire mail tender now.

278
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:58,000
At least I tried.

279
00:15:58,000 --> 00:15:59,000
Thanks. I'm sorry.

280
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Trying's all we ever do ever since we hit this town.

281
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
We should have never come here in the first place.

282
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
Guess a guy's got to be a native in this bird before a dam will give him a break.

283
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,000
No, give me that.

284
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Let's go home. I'm bushed.

285
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
I'm quail.

286
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
Let's move slow here.

287
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
I got a tip that this town's a cinch for a quick buck.

288
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
A whole dollar?

289
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
All we have to do is get some kind of pitch going at the fairground.

290
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Now's all we have to worry about finding the right thing.

291
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Skip it. I'm going to bed.

292
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
We'll pull out in the morning.

293
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
I'm loaded for action tonight.

294
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
I'll take a drive and see what I can pick up.

295
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,000
Bad chance.

296
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Remember, the car's half mine, so drive carefully.

297
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
You're half the front seat.

298
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
You won't be using your half-munch tonight.

299
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Wet head and dry look.

300
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
Oh, mouth, no action.

301
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
Ew.

302
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
No, I want to walk off my great expectations.

303
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Ew.

304
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
The wheels of progress.

305
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Or should I say the progress of a big wheel?

306
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
I resent that.

307
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
I can't believe I made those two guys.

308
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:02,000
The onion field.

309
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:07,000
I don't know about you guys, but I'm rooting for the eye creatures.

310
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
Hey, dim your cuff links.

311
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:19,000
Chip Douglas, you come in here this instant.

312
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
It's a cow sill.

313
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:30,000
Oh, well, it's the end of the world as we know it. I feel fine.

314
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
How about that, baby?

315
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Oh, Harold, when you kiss me...

316
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
Turn it off! Turn it off!

317
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
I just see explosions.

318
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:44,000
You know what? When he kisses her, I see explosions...

319
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
You know what? You are one sick mama-jama.

320
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:57,000
Looks like he walked right out of a J. Crew catalog.

321
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
(Siren wails)

322
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:05,000
# I'm never gonna give you up

323
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
# Never gonna let you down

324
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
# Never gonna fall... #

325
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:15,000
Oh, so bright.

326
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
Heh, heh, heh.

327
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
He looks like Roy Cohn.

328
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Hammer.

329
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,000
Huh, Mariah Carey's running through the woods.

330
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:33,000
OK, I can go now.

331
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,000
You sure it was out by the old Bailey house?

332
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,000
Sure, dead sure.

333
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
It was probably a plane going down.

334
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Anything could happen when these crazy electrical storms were around.

335
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
That's it. Low lightning. What else?

336
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
How dare you negate me?

337
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
You still believe the old Bailey house is haunted?

338
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
Maybe the ghosts have come back.

339
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,000
That old place was built before the Civil War.

340
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Could be.

341
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:57,000
Well, how's your recruiting coming along, Lieutenant?

342
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:58,000
Not bad.

343
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,000
Why don't you, uh, Shanghai a couple of them?

344
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Are you kidding?

345
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
Wonder what they've jabbering about.

346
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
Oh, art, science, critical theory, you know.

347
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
She dresses like a bobber.

348
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:13,000
I don't see any girls around, so what's keeping you Romeos so busy?

349
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
Jim just saw a spaceship.

350
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Only one?

351
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
With all the beer he drank, you'd think he'd see at least six.

352
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:20,000
(Laughs)

353
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
Drink this one.

354
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:23,000
I like gum.

355
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
I've heard the yellow ones myself.

356
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:28,000
Hey, speaking of, uh, yellow ones...

357
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:29,000
How's your tea?

358
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
Why don't you all go over and talk to that Air Force recruiting officer?

359
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:33,000
Yeah, he makes improper advances.

360
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,000
So that's what he is.

361
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
I've seen him hanging around, too.

362
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:37,000
That's what he says he is.

363
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
This place has all the ambiance of a Vietnamese restaurant.

364
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:43,000
Well, the boys were just kidding about seeing a spaceship, Lieutenant.

365
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
Everyone knows there's no such thing.

366
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:48,000
How many times do we have to deny it before people will believe us?

367
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Well, excuse me, fly boy.

368
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
Oh, I'll get back to you when I think of an insult.

369
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:53,000
Better watch out.

370
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
The way things are going, he may be giving some of you orders next month.

371
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:57,000
Man, I'm hooked.

372
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
They're dragging me in feet first.

373
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
Don't you have a date with Susan tonight?

374
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:02,000
I do. I'm picking her up at 9.30.

375
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
She's, uh, fussing with a new dress.

376
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:05,000
You can take her with us.

377
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:08,000
We know you're waiting for her old man to get out of the house.

378
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
What kind of dress takes that long to get into?

379
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Waitress, I think we're done.

380
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:14,000
Susan's got a lot of the right things to put in the right places.

381
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:15,000
Yeah.

382
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:16,000
And they're all mine.

383
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:17,000
Ooh!

384
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:18,000
Ooh!

385
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:19,000
Right, we'll see.

386
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
I admire him.

387
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
We now return to Macon County line.

388
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
Do-do-do-do-do-do-do-do-do.

389
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:36,000
Go and get me a piece of bottom land.

390
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
Jesse Helms in retirement.

391
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Yay! Kill all the characters! Woo!

392
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:47,000
Another carload of them blasted smooches on my property.

393
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
I'll get the law after 'em.

394
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:52,000
Thank you for providing a running commentary.

395
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:56,000
I can't seem to get the stink out.

396
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:59,000
The wind song stays in my car.

397
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
Look, little Billy left his toy on the lawn.

398
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
There's a twig in front of him.

399
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:07,000
Run! There's a snap-tight bottle in the woods.

400
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:12,000
What are you, Jamie Lee Curtis?

401
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:16,000
Little red shev that.

402
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:22,000
Ron Howard pops the clutch and tells the world to eat his dust.

403
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
Boy, sure is dark tonight, huh?

404
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
Ooh, dark.

405
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:33,000
Look, people have a right to drive on the road, old man. Come on.

406
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:38,000
I'll fix 'em.

407
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:41,000
And I'm let to maybe continental breakfast, but that's it.

408
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:45,000
They ain't gonna work on Maggie's farm no more.

409
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
Tiger!

410
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:54,000
Darling, your head is full.

411
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:55,000
Tonight as usual.

412
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
Why not? We're only young ones.

413
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
Besides, I'm gonna show you the moon.

414
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:00,000
God, no, please, stay seated.

415
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
I can see you anytime.

416
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:03,000
You can.

417
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Yes.

418
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,000
Ooh, she's got a praying mantis.

419
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,000
All right, go get 'em, Tiger.

420
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Huh.

421
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:17,000
Hey, whoa. Take it easy.

422
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:20,000
Not so fast. I'm out of breath.

423
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:23,000
Calling Susan Faludi. Calling Susan Faludi.

424
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
I'm eloping with you tonight.

425
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
That's proof enough, isn't it?

426
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
It sure is, seeing how your father hates me.

427
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
Don't get a fool that bad.

428
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:34,000
(engine starts)

429
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:46,000
Whoa, Tommy, that is one sweet ride.

430
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:47,000
Well, hop in.

431
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:50,000
Oh, yeah, no thanks. Your driving makes me scared.

432
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
Oh, we won't drive anywhere.

433
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
I thought we could be like the teeners in the movie.

434
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,000
You know, sit and talk for a while, then maybe make out.

435
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
Oh, okay, that sounds like fun. What?

436
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
Make out. You know, smooch, like in the movie.

437
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
Okay, now let's figure this out.

438
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,000
First you scooch over close to me, then tilt your head back and moan.

439
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
Oh, my God, no!

440
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
Ah!

441
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:14,000
Huh, wonder what's eating him.

442
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:15,000
Hi, Tom.

443
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:16,000
Hi.

444
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
Hey, sweet ride.

445
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
Thanks. Hey, you want to make out?

446
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
(laughs)

447
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:25,000
Oh, come on, please, as a friend, I've never been kissed before, except on my arm,

448
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
and I've never tasted lips.

449
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
Commercial sign in two minutes.

450
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
Say, magic voice.

451
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
No, Tom, I won't.

452
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,000
Well, actually, I can't. I have no physical form.

453
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:37,000
But you do know about it.

454
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:40,000
Making out? Sure, it's nothing new.

455
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
Well, I guess I've heard of it before, too.

456
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
It just never struck me like it did today.

457
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:45,000
It seemed brand new.

458
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:48,000
And, well, I have so many questions, like, why?

459
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:49,000
Why does one make out?

460
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:52,000
And once you've started, how do you know when you're done?

461
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:55,000
Now, I consider it a greeting, like a firm handshake, only with the lips.

462
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:56,000
Am I right?

463
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
Oh, yeah, and is it necessary to use the lips?

464
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
I mean, could I kiss with my forehead or my elbow?

465
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
And are the teeth involved?

466
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
And where the heck do you store your tongue?

467
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:05,000
Another thing, be a sucker blow.

468
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:08,000
Is it under some sort of strict rabbinical supervision or something?

469
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
And here's a puzzler, who kisses whom and when?

470
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
I mean, being a robot, can I kiss animals?

471
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
Or am I confined to a single species?

472
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:18,000
Oh, yeah, I can't pucker. Is that a handicap?

473
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:23,000
How can a man in a single lifetime know everything there is to know about making out?

474
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,000
You know something, mister? You ask too many questions.

475
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:27,000
Well, I'm only complaining--

476
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:28,000
Mwah!

477
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
You know, I've always wanted to do that.

478
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
Hmm.

479
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:43,000
I'm sorry, Tom. I was just teasing.

480
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:44,000
Oh.

481
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:45,000
Mike, we've made it.

482
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
What a surprise. He lives in a shabby furnace room.

483
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,000
Travis, Bickel had a better room than this.

484
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:54,000
Wake up, I said. Mike, we've got it made. We're millionaires.

485
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:57,000
Really? I thought we were oily drifters.

486
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:00,000
But this is different. It's big.

487
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Now, look, I'm not trying to give you a pitch, but get this.

488
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
Term insurance.

489
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
You and me are going to put on an exhibition--

490
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:10,000
No!

491
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:15,000
--first and only, truly authentic spaceship.

492
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:18,000
You woke me up to tell me that?

493
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:20,000
Told you it was big, didn't I?

494
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
Well, I've seen one.

495
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
Just like all these jokers have been saying.

496
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:28,000
It's big and round and glows sort of funny-like.

497
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:29,000
Oh, that's the big boy sign.

498
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
And gets on this funny sound like--

499
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:34,000
[growling]

500
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:35,000
That's great.

501
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,000
Police! Police!

502
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:40,000
Like that, huh?

503
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:41,000
Yeah.

504
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:44,000
And it landed over somewhere near that old house that's supposed to be haunted.

505
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
Great, isn't it?

506
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
Not what you want.

507
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
All right, get in.

508
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:55,000
I forgot to ask you, how was your day?

